<attribute name="guid" nodeId="c56c096f">_0S__YALZEeSFtNrJ4VAzEw</attribute>
<attribute name="presentationName" nodeId="2eba9ad4">Unit test configuration</attribute>
<attribute name="briefDescription" nodeId="a4f57e72"></attribute>
<attribute name="suppressed" nodeId="3aa65181">false</attribute>
<attribute name="orderingGuide" nodeId="b3577bca"></attribute>
<attribute name="variabilityType" nodeId="f43982d6">na</attribute>
<attribute name="sectionName" nodeId="1aa0ba47"></attribute>
<attribute name="sectionDescription" nodeId="71ffe968">&lt;h5&gt;
    1. Write unit test scripts
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Develop unit test scripts based on functional requirements, including expected outcomes in the script. Unit test
    scripts form the building blocks to create assembly, integration and user acceptance tests, therefore test scripts
    should be detailed and robust so they can used by the test workstream to develop other tests.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
    2. Define unit test data
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Define required data elements for successful unit testing of either the code or configured object. For the purpose of
    unit testing, the data needed will be created.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
    3. Review and sign off unit test scripts
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Review unit test scripts with the test workstream and functional teams (including key client stakeholders). Obtain
    sign-off from these parties before proceeding.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
    4. Perform unit tests and log results
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Execute unit test according to approved test scripts and record results, including whether expected (and desired)
    results were achieved. Screenshots and key output should be attached to the test and form documentation to support
    approval of test results by the test workstream.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
    5. Fix defects and retest
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Resolve defects (i.e., configuration changes or code fixes) and retest. Record test results including whether the
    object produced the expected (and desired) result. Repeat tasks 4 and 5 until all major defects have been resolved.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
